 minimums. To decide which debt to pay first, make minimum payments across every balance, send your extra monthly budget to your target debt, and roll each freed minimum payment into the next balance once an account hits zero. The avalanche strategy targets the balance carrying the highest annual percentage rate (APR), while the snowball method targets the smallest balance first to give faster early wins. This calculator starts preloaded with a store card, a credit card, and a personal loan so you can immediately see the three-plan comparison. Replace the rows with your own balances, interest rates, minimum payments, and extra monthly budget to compare your payoff dates. Share Interactive — edit any field Snowball vs Avalanche on Your Numbers In our internal modeling, we compared avalanche, snowball, and minimum-payment schedules across $19,500 in consumer debt with a $200 extra monthly payment to measure the gap in total interest and completion time. The avalanche strategy clears all three balances in 34 months with $4,783.63 in total interest. The snowball strategy clears the debts in 35 months with $5,120.19 in interest, costing $336.56 more. Paying only minimums stretches the repayment period to 91 months and accumulates $12,291.39 in interest, which is $7,507.76 more interest and 57 additional months compared to avalanche. The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB) describes the highest interest rate method as focusing extra payments on your costliest balances to reduce total borrowing costs over the long run. In contrast, the CFPB defines the snowball method as paying off your smallest balance first so you eliminate individual obligations sooner. In our preloaded example, the snowball method eliminates the $2,500 store card in month 10, whereas the avalanche method eliminates its first balance (the $6,000 credit card at 27.99% APR) in month 22. Snowball clears its first debt 12 months sooner than avalanche clears its first, which is a faster early win, and it costs $336.56 in additional interest. The financial difference between avalanche and snowball depends entirely on your rate spread. When all your debts share the same interest rate, the choice disappears. In our modeling of two debts at an identical 18% APR ($3,000 and $7,000 with a $150 extra payment), both avalanche and snowball finish in exactly 30 months and cost identical interest of $2,464.56. When your smallest balance also happens to carry your highest APR, the two methods follow the exact same payoff order. Modeling a $1,500 balance at 26% APR alongside a $9,000 loan at 9% APR with $100 extra per month produces an identical 35-month timeline, $1,611.43 in total interest, and a month-12 payoff for the first account across both strategies. How the Calculator Works The calculator runs a deterministic month-by-month mathematical simulation to project your payoff dates across all open accounts. Each month, the engine adds interest to every active account by multiplying the current balance by its APR divided by 12. Next, it subtracts the required minimum payment from each account, capping that payment at the remaining balance. Finally, the tool sums your original minimum payments, your extra monthly payment, and any month-one lump sum into a unified monthly budget, directing every unallocated dollar to your target debt before spilling excess funds down the order. Rolling payments forward is the core mechanism that speeds up both strategies. When you fully pay off an account, that account's required minimum payment does not disappear from your budget. Instead, the engine automatically rolls that freed payment into the extra payment pool for the next debt in line. If a lender sets a minimum payment that falls below the account's monthly accrued interest, the calculator warns you that the balance is not shrinking on that minimum alone. For an individual account payoff or detailed amortization steps, use our credit card payoff calculator to evaluate single-card minimums. The model assumes fixed interest rates, on-time payments, no new charges or penalties, and consistent monthly funding throughout the payoff timeline. If you are deciding between paying accounts individually or replacing them with a single personal loan, our debt consolidation calculator evaluates taking out one new loan rather than ordering payments across existing accounts. Worked Example: Three Debts and $200 Extra To see the math in action, examine the baseline scenario preloaded into the calculator. This profile includes three common consumer obligations totaling $19,500 in principal and $515 in base minimum payments. Adding $200 per month raises the total monthly debt budget to $715. Debt Name Starting Balance APR Minimum Monthly Payment Store card $2,500 19.99% $75 Credit card $6,000 27.99% $160 Personal loan $11,000 11.00% $280 Here is how the three strategies perform when handling these balances: Payoff Strategy Debt-Free Timeline Total Interest Paid Total Amount Paid Account Payoff Order Avalanche 34 months $4,783.63 $24,283.63 Credit card (mo. 22), Store card (mo. 26), Personal loan (mo. 34) Snowball 35 months $5,120.19 $24,620.19 Store card (mo. 10), Credit card (mo. 27), Personal loan (mo. 35) Minimums Only 91 months $12,291.39 $31,791.39 Personal loan (mo. 49), Store card (mo. 50), Credit card (mo. 91) Avalanche minimizes total interest charges whenever interest rates differ across accounts, while snowball costs slightly more in exchange for faster early eliminations. With equal interest rates or when your smallest balance also carries your highest rate, the choice between the two methods produces identical costs. This payoff ordering applies to unsecured debts and installment loans that you can prepay without penalty. Our recommendation would change if an account carries a prepayment penalty, if you have access to a 0% promotional rate, or if you hold only a single account. This multi-debt calculator is not intended for someone carrying only one balance, who should use the credit card payoff calculator instead. It is also not suitable for debts with active 0% promotional periods that should not be prioritized ahead of high-interest accounts. Frequently asked questions Which debt should I pay off first? Pay off the debt with the highest APR first if you want to pay the least total interest, which is the avalanche method. Choose your smallest balance first if you want faster early wins, which is the snowball method. Regardless of strategy, always make minimum payments on all other accounts. How long will it take to pay off all my debts? Payoff time depends on your total balances, interest rates, and extra payments. In our baseline $19,500 example, paying only minimums takes 91 months. Adding $200 extra per month cuts that timeline to 34 months under avalanche and 35 months under snowball, saving nearly five years of payments. Does adding a lump sum payment speed up debt payoff? Applying a lump sum immediately reduces principal, lowering monthly interest accrual across all remaining months. In our model, adding a $2,000 month-one lump sum to the sample debts reduces avalanche repayment from 34 to 30 months and drops total interest from $4,783.63 to $3,323.03, saving $1,460.60 in interest. Can I use this calculator for biweekly payments? The calculator runs on monthly cycles, but you can approximate biweekly payments by adjusting your extra monthly input. Because 26 biweekly half-payments equal 13 full payments per year, divide your total monthly debt payment by 12 and enter that figure as your extra monthly payment to simulate the extra annual payment. Can I include a car loan, student loan, or HELOC? Yes, you can include any debt that has a fixed balance, an APR, and a monthly minimum payment. Keep in mind that car loans and home equity lines of credit are secured by collateral, and some installment loans carry prepayment penalties that alter the financial advantage of accelerating payoff. Why might my lender's payoff date differ from this calculator? Some lenders add interest daily, while this calculator adds it monthly using APR divided by 12. Differences also occur if your APR is variable, if the lender charges account maintenance fees, or if your actual payment dates vary from month to month. Can I track debt payoff in Excel or Google Sheets? You can replicate this logic in a spreadsheet by creating a row for each month and tracking interest, minimums, extra payments, and rolling balances across columns. This calculator runs those monthly allocation loops automatically, showing instant comparison totals without requiring manual formula maintenance or schedule rebuilding. Should I pay off debt or invest extra cash? Compare your guaranteed debt interest rate against your expected after-tax investment return. Read our guide on whether to pay off debt or invest to evaluate your options. Sources We prioritize primary sources for rules, formulas, rates, limits, and definitions. See our calculator methodology and editorial policy . Consumer Financial Protection Bureau: How to reduce your debt Plan the bigger picture Would one consolidation loan cost less? → Pay off one card: time and interest → Debt snowball vs avalanche → Pay off debt or invest? → Build a cushion so debt doesn't return → ModernWallet Free, accurate financial calculators — auto loans, mortgages, retirement, investing, rentals, and net worth. No signup, instant results. 📣 Advertise With Us Calculators All calculators Auto Loan Mortgage Retirement Investing Portfolio Real Estate Net Worth Budget Tax Resolution Estate Planning Probate Elder Care Trump Account 529 College Savings Coast FIRE Business Loan Payoff Personal Loan Interest Per Day Taxable vs. Tax-Deferred Credit Card Payoff IUL Merchant Cash Advance Invoice Factoring Business Line of Credit PTO Cash-Out Zakat Life Insurance Needs Self-Employment Tax Operating Cash Flow Mileage Deduction Cash Conversion Cycle Freelance Rate HELOC S-Corp Tax Comme